Based on the current repository, I'd consider these areas:

1. Fonts — worth upgrading

You currently have a strong technical typography direction, but you can get a more distinctive identity with free fonts.

Good combinations:

Role	Font	Why
Main UI	Geist	Extremely clean, modern SaaS/product feel
Technical UI	IBM Plex Sans	Technical without looking gimmicky
Headings	Space Grotesk	More character and stronger display hierarchy
Code	JetBrains Mono	Excellent for commands, payloads, terminal content
Alternative	DM Sans + JetBrains Mono	Clean and understated

My preference for SecCraft would be:

Space Grotesk + Inter/Geist + JetBrains Mono

But don't use 3–4 decorative fonts. Two families + monospace is enough.

2. Advanced CSS — definitely

There is a lot you can do without adding a heavy animation library.

For example:

Dynamic border lighting

Instead of static card borders:

.card {
  position: relative;
  isolation: isolate;
}

.card::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -1px;
  border-radius: inherit;
  background: conic-gradient(
    from var(--angle),
    transparent 0deg,
    rgba(83, 215, 209, .4) 40deg,
    transparent 80deg
  );
  z-index: -1;
  animation: rotate-border 8s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes rotate-border {
  to {
    --angle: 360deg;
  }
}

Used sparingly, this could look excellent on major interactive surfaces.

3. Interactive “instrument” effects

This is probably more appropriate for SecCraft than generic glassmorphism.

Examples:

cursor-reactive radial light
subtle grid distortion
animated telemetry lines
scanning indicators
progress traces
signal-strength visualizations
packet-flow animations
waveform-like decorations
topology lines
network-node diagrams

For example, your dashboard could have a subtle background that behaves like a security monitoring instrument, rather than just a gradient.

That would reinforce the product's identity.

4. Framer Motion — you already have it

You don't need another animation framework.

You're already using Framer Motion extensively.

Instead, I'd make the existing animation system more sophisticated.

For example:

Page transition
       ↓
Section reveal
       ↓
Card stagger
       ↓
Interactive hover
       ↓
State transition
       ↓
Completion animation

Different animation types should communicate different things.

A completed challenge could have:

VALIDATED
    ↓
evidence collected
    ↓
+150 XP
    ↓
module progress updates

with a short coordinated animation.

That's much more valuable than making every card float on hover.

5. Use Lottie / Rive selectively

There are free resources available here.

But I wouldn't put animated illustrations everywhere.

Good use cases:

Achievement unlocked

Lab completed

Challenge validated

Empty state

First-time onboarding

Rive is particularly interesting if you want interactive visual states rather than simple animations.

